ine through (2,3) and (5,1): slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3 ≈ -0.666..., not -1, not 0, not ∞. So L3 covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\nNow we have left points: (2,4), (3,1), (3,3), (4,2). That's 4 points. We have one sunny line left (L4). Can we cover all four with one line? No, a line can cover at most 2 points unless they are collinear. Are any three of these collinear? Let's check possibilities.\n\nPoints: (2,4), (3,1), (3,3), (4,2). Check if any three are collinear with slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\n- (2,4) and (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. Does (4,2) lie on that line? The line through (2,4) and (3,1) has equation: slope -3, passing through (2,4): y-4 = -3(x-2) => y = -3x + 6 + 4 = -3x + 10. At x=4, y = -12+10 = -2, not 2. So (4,2) not on line. At x=3, y = -9+10 = 1, matches (3,1). So only those two points.\n\n- (2,4) and (3,3): slope = (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, which is slope -1, not allowed for sunny lines. So cannot use that line as sunny.\n\n- (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\n- (3,1) and (3,3): vertical, not allowed.\n\n- (3,1) and (4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, sunny. Does (2,4) lie on that line? Equation: y-1 = 1(x-3) => y = x - 2. At x=2, y=0, not 4. So no. At (3,3): y=1? No.\n\n- (3,3) and (4,2): slope = (2-3)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we cannot cover all four remaining points with one sunny line. So this particular arrangement fails. But maybe there is a different arrangement of lines that works.\n\nWe need to find a covering of the 10 points with 4 sunny lines. Let's try to think more systematically. The points are arranged in a triangular shape. The non-sunny line we used is vertical x=1. Could we use a different non-sunny line, like a diagonal x+y=6? That diagonal passes through (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's 5 points. That would be a non-sunny line covering many points. Then we would have 4 sunny lines left to cover the remaining points: (1,1)-(1,4) (since (1,5) covered), (2,1)-(2,3) (since (2,4) covered), (3,1)-(